{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"a20d547acd6ed1e2","type":"group","x":-4110,"y":1910,"width":1390,"height":2050,"label":"Neuroplastic factors"},
		{"id":"99a23654f1116c15","type":"group","x":1440,"y":780,"width":2116,"height":780,"label":"Current beliefs on Neuroglia"},
		{"id":"836b80d9b92bdc60","type":"group","x":1983,"y":1840,"width":1030,"height":320,"label":"Modern Research on Neuroglia"},
		{"id":"20deb1642276f3fd","type":"text","text":"### Slower Component\n- 0.2-2.5 mm per day\n- Carries neurofilaments and α- and β-tubulin subunits of microtubules.","x":-539,"y":1840,"width":250,"height":219},
		{"id":"b046141bea77f77f","type":"text","text":"### Faster Component\n- 0.4-5 mm per day\n- Carries clathrin, actin, and actin-binding proteins, enzymes","x":-919,"y":1841,"width":250,"height":218},
		{"id":"38e5999143e8d0b9","type":"text","text":"- Only in anterograde direction\n- Carries cytosolic and cytoskeletal proteins (Neurofilaments & Microfilaments)\n\n##### Has 2 kinetic components\n- Slower component travels at 0.2 to 2.5 mm per\nday\n- Faster component (speed = 2 x slower component)","x":-794,"y":1377,"width":380,"height":313},
		{"id":"f0b792359a2689bb","type":"text","text":"#### Slow Axonal Transport Carries Cytosolic Proteins and Elements of the Cytoskeleton","x":-794,"y":1213,"width":380,"height":84},
		{"id":"04aa892c2bd9cba7","type":"text","text":"## Protein Particles and Organelles Are Actively Transported Along the Axon and Dendrites","x":-1436,"y":960,"width":732,"height":98},
		{"id":"743f379b37cc68c2","type":"text","text":"##### Both electrically and chemically excitable via ion channels and receptors","x":-1680,"y":600,"width":250,"height":100},
		{"id":"ef106df3dc6fb807","type":"text","text":"#### Fast Axonal Transport \nCarries Membranous Organelles\n- Synaptic vesicle precursors\n- Large dense-core vesicles\n- Mitochondria\n- Elements of smooth endoplasmic reticulum\n- Proteins dissociated from these organelles via collision\n- Microtubules act as molecular pipelines for transport","x":-1726,"y":1213,"width":380,"height":331},
		{"id":"b6ab9a2f961365b2","type":"text","text":"#### Retrograde ","x":-1471,"y":1660,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"e04dab169012f3e2","type":"text","text":"Neurons communicate with different and distant parts of the body rapidly via 2 main properties - ","x":-1901,"y":400,"width":362,"height":83},
		{"id":"a889a72a2d3c2e68","type":"text","text":"###### Computation and communication cells of the body.","x":-1901,"y":240,"width":362,"height":60},
		{"id":"6bc0c44cd98485a7","type":"text","text":"\n##### High degree of morphological and functional asymmetry","x":-2000,"y":600,"width":250,"height":100},
		{"id":"a6589448b2668530","type":"text","text":"## Structure\n\n![[Pasted Image 20251208115225_422.png]]\n\nRef: https://www.healthline.com/health/neurons","x":-2617,"y":960,"width":560,"height":520},
		{"id":"3f2687f4d15da829","type":"text","text":"#### Anterograde","x":-1860,"y":1660,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"ebe4d3b39245aa45","type":"text","text":"- 1/2 to 2/3rds speed of Anterograde fast transport\n- Endosomes generated by endocytic activity at nerve endings\n- Mitochondria \n- Elements of the endoplasmic reticulum.","x":-1471,"y":1880,"width":250,"height":240},
		{"id":"fef1babe818fb9c7","type":"text","text":"- Depends critically on ATP\n- Not affected by inhibitors of protein synthesis","x":-1860,"y":1880,"width":250,"height":100},
		{"id":"936ed073746def20","type":"text","text":"Retrograde fast transport also delivers **signals that regulate gene expression** in the neuron’s nucleus (eg. Transport of transcription factors)\n\nHence, also important in **nerve**\n**regeneration and axon regrowth**","x":-1497,"y":2160,"width":303,"height":200},
		{"id":"668dc9acbd8b33ac","type":"text","text":"#### Astrocytes\n1. **Types:** Fibrous (white matter) and protoplasmic (gray matter).\n2. **Functions:**\n\t- Structural support and scaffolding for neurons.\n\t- Form **blood–brain barrier** (end-feet cover capillaries).\n\t- Regulate extracellular ions (e.g., K⁺ buffering).\n\t- Uptake of excess neurotransmitters (e.g., glutamate).\n\t- Involved in **repair & scar formation** after CNS injury.\n\t- Metabolic support: supply lactate to neurons.","x":1460,"y":800,"width":450,"height":387},
		{"id":"98fd9e7dce751013","type":"text","text":"## **CNS Glia**\n\n","x":1986,"y":540,"width":140,"height":60},
		{"id":"8c1a6b6a16040905","type":"text","text":"### Emerging / Modern Views — Glia are not just “support cells”\nAs per ongoing research, Glial cells might also be involved in signaling, **but neurons are still considered the basis of this function.**\n- Glia influence synaptic function, plasticity, and even information processing: glial-neuronal signaling is a major field of modern neuroscience. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S089662730300607X)","x":2000,"y":1859,"width":463,"height":280},
		{"id":"ad6453dc1c890c0f","type":"text","text":"###### Current beliefs - Mainly play a supporting role for nervous tissue. ","x":2314,"y":240,"width":362,"height":60},
		{"id":"1135827ba25bac26","type":"text","text":"# Glial cells","x":2370,"y":80,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"b27eb7c84e849200","type":"text","text":"- Some theoretical neuroscience work argues we should see the brain as a **_neuron-glia network_**, not just neuronal networks. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.10719)","x":2611,"y":1860,"width":382,"height":125},
		{"id":"429633baeb70371c","type":"text","text":"## **PNS Glia**\n\n\n\n","x":2866,"y":540,"width":140,"height":60},
		{"id":"417a3aace3faa53b","type":"text","text":"- One recent theoretical framework describes astrocytes as “contextual gates” [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.09906)","x":2611,"y":2067,"width":382,"height":73},
		{"id":"6c5b769989bffcc9","type":"text","text":"\n\n\n\n#### **Ependymal Cells**\n1. **Structure:** Ciliated cuboidal/columnar epithelium lining ventricles and central canal.\n2. **Functions:**\n\t- Produce and circulate **cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)** via ciliary movement.\n\t- Form **choroid plexus epithelium** (CSF secretion).\n\t- Barrier between CSF and CNS tissue (partial)","x":1460,"y":1260,"width":450,"height":280},
		{"id":"f7b5e080aa0feca4","type":"text","text":"- Length: Variable\n- Diameter: 10 nm in diameter,\n- Belong to the intermediate filament (IF) family.\n\t- Intermediate filaments are a shared cytoskeletal system across cell types.\n\t- Different cells express **different IF proteins** (eg.  cytokeratins of epithelial cells (hair and nails), glial fibrillary acidic protein in astrocytes, and desmin in muscle), but they are - \n\t\t- structurally related\n\t\t- genetically homologous  \n\t\t- functionally analogous\n - Neurofilaments are stable and almost totally polymerized in the cell.\n","x":-3628,"y":2078,"width":430,"height":402},
		{"id":"f7693a15e0b7f1d2","type":"text","text":"- Length: ≤ 0.1mm\n- Diameter: ~25nm, Inner lumen ~ 15nm\n- Composed of spirally arranged protofilaments, \n\t- Parallel pairs of α- and β-tubulin subunits arranged longitudinally along the microtubule.\n\t- Tubulins are encoded by a multigene family. At least six genes code the α- and β-subunits. \n\t- Because of the expression of the different genes or post transcriptional modifications more than 20 isoforms of tubulin are present in the brain.\n- Polarity: \n\t- Negative end -> center of the cell \n\t- Positive end -> periphery, to the dendrites and axon.\n","x":-4090,"y":2078,"width":430,"height":402},
		{"id":"f01e63ce51b1d487","type":"text","text":"#### Microtubule growth\n\n- Addition of GTP to positive end\n- GTP -> GDP + P<sub>i</sub>\n\n##### Stoppage of Growth\n- Positive end is capped by a GDP bound tubulin monomer.\n- This monomer has low affinity for microtubule polymer.\n- This leads to depolymerization.\n\n### Stability\n\n- **In dividing cells, Microtubules undergo rapid cycles of polymerization and depolymerization.**\n\n- In mature dendrites and axons they are much more stable. \n\t- This stability is caused by microtubule-associated proteins (MAPs) that promote the oriented polymerization and assembly of the tubulin polymers.\n\t- In Alzheimer disease and some other degenerative disorders tau proteins are modified and abnormally polymerized, forming a characteristic lesion called the neurofibrillary tangle (Box 4–1).\n\nNote: MAPs in axons = tau & MAP3, in dendrites = MAP2\n\n\n","x":-4090,"y":2540,"width":430,"height":800},
		{"id":"9cd444b42372def5","type":"text","text":"#### **Microglia**\n1. **Origin:** Mesodermal/monocyte lineage.\n2. **Functions:**\n\t- Immune defense in CNS (resident macrophages).\n\t- Phagocytose debris, pathogens, and dead neurons.\n\t- Secrete cytokines during inflammation.\n\t- Participate in synaptic pruning during development.","x":1970,"y":1260,"width":448,"height":280},
		{"id":"7e9c14299946c95a","type":"text","text":"#### **Oligodendrocytes**\n1. **Structure:** Small cell body with multiple processes.\n2. **Functions:**\n\t- <span style=\"color:#e0de71;\"> Myelinate <strong>multiple</strong> CNS axons (1 oligodendrocyte -> many axons)</span> \n\t- Provide metabolic support to axons.\n\t- Important for rapid saltatory conduction.\n","x":1970,"y":800,"width":448,"height":240},
		{"id":"93af7bba5aabc5fd","type":"text","text":"![[image-37.png]]","x":3223,"y":1676,"width":626,"height":647},
		{"id":"55fbccb6a6e4c9e6","type":"text","text":"#### **Overall Functions:**\n- Structural support for neurons.\n- Myelination → faster impulse conduction.\n- Maintain extracellular environment (ions, neurotransmitters).\n- Immune defense and repair.\n- Facilitate synaptic function and nutrient supply.","x":3082,"y":1297,"width":454,"height":243},
		{"id":"fd0197a6a5b9c231","type":"text","text":"#### **Schwann Cells**\n1. **Types:** Myelinating and non-myelinating.\n2. **Functions:**\n    - <span style=\"color:#e0de71;\"> Myelinate <strong>single</strong> PNS axon (1:1 relationship)</span> \n    - Promote **axon regeneration** after injury (form bands of Büngner).\n     - Support neuronal metabolism.\n     - Insulate axons for [[SN - Saltatory Conduction of Myelinated Neuron|Saltatory Conduction]].","x":2586,"y":800,"width":450,"height":260},
		{"id":"09578336f47a2a61","type":"text","text":"\n\n#### **Satellite Cells**\n1. **Location:** Surround neuron cell bodies in sensory, sympathetic, and parasympathetic ganglia.\n2. **Functions:**\n    -  Provide **structural support** and nutrition. \n    -  Regulate extracellular environment around ganglionic neurons.\n    - Possibly modulate neuronal signaling.","x":3086,"y":800,"width":450,"height":260},
		{"id":"2d1c06348fa7488c","type":"text","text":"#### Dendrites","x":-2462,"y":1600,"width":245,"height":60},
		{"id":"99d4cdebce970c96","type":"text","text":"#### Cell Body (Soma)","x":-2462,"y":1700,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"00d7dbbcbfc6d442","type":"text","text":"#### Axon","x":-2462,"y":1800,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"16901ab4af59a674","type":"text","text":"#### Nodes of Ranvier","x":-2462,"y":1900,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"16e33e6b0cb61c99","type":"text","text":"#### Myelin Sheath","x":-2462,"y":2000,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"fdaabdb01a382362","type":"text","text":"- Length: Variable but short\n- Diameter: 3–7 nm, thinnest of the three\n- Made up of two strands of polymerized globular actin monomers (β and γ actin), each bearing an ATP or ADP molecule, wound into a double-stranded helix. \n- Highly conserved intercellularly and even interspecies.\n","x":-3170,"y":2081,"width":430,"height":198},
		{"id":"5764ebb36fd5b089","type":"text","text":"#### Axon Terminal w/ synaptic knobs","x":-2462,"y":2100,"width":250,"height":82},
		{"id":"39facc6af007f983","type":"text","text":"- **Concentrated at the cell’s periphery** in the cortical cytoplasm just underneath the plasmalemma, where they **form a dense network with many** **actin-binding proteins** (eg, spectrin-fodrin, ankyrin, talin, and actinin). \n- This matrix plays a key role in the dynamic function of the cell’s periphery, such as\n\t- Motility of growth cones (the growing tips of axons) during development, \n\t- Generation of specialized microdomains at the cell surface, \n\t- Formation of pre- and postsynaptic morphological specializations.","x":-3170,"y":2340,"width":430,"height":360},
		{"id":"d304a5442fc2a022","type":"text","text":"# Modern Research and Simulations","x":-2640,"y":4450,"width":492,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"b84d911ddd22688d","type":"text","text":"##### Growing Living Rat Neurons To Play... DOOM? (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bEXefdbQDjw)","x":-2624,"y":4660,"width":460,"height":140},
		{"id":"b515ab7046d0faa9","type":"text","text":"# Nervous tissue is made up of neurons and glial cells","x":-795,"y":-180,"width":579,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"66c990f74f5bf6d2","type":"text","text":"## Golgi staining of Neurons, 1873 \n![[image-36.png]]\n\nRef: [www.frontiersin.org - The Original Histological Slides of Camillo Golgi and His Discoveries on Neuronal Structure](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/neuroanatomy/articles/10.3389/fnana.2019.00003/full)","x":-2840,"y":-97,"width":440,"height":415},
		{"id":"05202212a980f2bb","type":"text","text":"### Microfilaments","x":-3080,"y":1930,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"20345be02f5c3c9b","type":"text","text":"#### Microfilament growth\nMicrofilaments also undergo cycles of polymerization and depolymerization.\n\n- At any one time approximately half the total actin in a cell can exist as unpolymerized monomers.\n- State of actin is controlled by binding proteins, which facilitate assembly and limit polymer length by capping the rapidly growing end of the filament or by severing it. \n- Other binding proteins crosslink or bundle microfilaments.\n- Motors used by the actin filaments, the myosins, also mediate other types of cell motility, including extension of the cell’s processes, and the translocation of membranous organelles from the bulk cytoplasm to the region adjacent to the plasma membrane. \n#### The dynamic state of microtubules and microfilaments permits a mature neuron to retract old axons and dendrites and extend new ones. \n#### This feature is called *Neuroplasticity* is thought to be a major factor in changes of synaptic connections and efficacy, and therefore cellular mechanisms of long-term memory and learning. ","x":-3170,"y":2780,"width":430,"height":740},
		{"id":"3a511b3a9060abe8","type":"text","text":"#### Because the microtubules and actin filaments are polar, each motor drives its organelle cargo in only one direction.\n\nNot so imp: Microtubules are arranged in parallel in the axon with positive ends pointing away from the cell body and negative ends facing the cell body.\nThis regular orientation permits some organelles to move toward nerve endings and others to move away from nerve endings, the direction being determined by the specific type of molecule motor, thus maintaining the distinctive distribution of axonal organelles (Figure 4–7). \nIn dendrites, however, microtubules with opposite polarities are mixed together, explaining why the organelles of the cell body and dendrites are similar.","x":-3693,"y":3600,"width":560,"height":320},
		{"id":"a054be5d61c73b50","type":"text","text":"# The  nervous system is a \"clump\" of systematically connected neurons which change over time.","x":-3802,"y":4440,"width":779,"height":80,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"cbfa59e6a3902db7","type":"text","text":"Made through millions of years of tinkering by evolution gave us this structure today what is known as the brain.","x":-3650,"y":4720,"width":475,"height":80},
		{"id":"fc0987562c8ef693","type":"text","text":"### **A. Evolutionary Origin**\n\n- **Single-celled life → Excitable membranes:** Early unicellular organisms developed voltage-gated ion channels for survival (detecting environment, escaping predators).\n- **Multicellularity → Specialized signaling cells:** As organisms grew, some cells specialized in rapid communication → proto-neurons.\n- **Division of labor:** Supporting cells emerged as ion-buffering and metabolic assistants → proto-glia.\n\n### **B. Neuronal Development (Ontogeny)**\n\n- **Neurogenesis:**  \n    Neural stem cells → neuroblasts \n- Myelination → faster impulse conduction.\n- Maintain extracellular environment (ions, neurotransmitters).→ differentiated neurons.\n- **Migration:**  \n    Neurons travel along radial glia to find their final position.\n- **Axon pathfinding:**  \n    Growth cones navigate via attractants/repellents (e.g., Netrin, Slit).\n- **Synaptogenesis:**  \n    Neurons establish trillions of connections; activity-dependent refinement prunes excess synapses.\n- **Mature characteristics:**\n    - Highly polarized (dendrites vs axon)\n    - Electrochemical signaling (action potentials, neurotransmitters)\n    - Synaptic plasticity (LTP/LTD)\n\n### **C. Glial Development and Roles**\n\n- **Astrocytes:**  \n    Guide migration, regulate extracellular ions, provide metabolic support, modulate synapses.\n    \n- **Oligodendrocytes / Schwann cells:**  \n    Myelination → increased conduction velocity; metabolic support for axons.\n    \n- **Microglia:**  \n    Immune surveillance, synaptic pruning, injury response.\n    \n- **Ependymal cells:**  \n    CSF production and circulation.\n    \n\n### **D. Key Contrast: Neurons vs Glia**\n\n- **Neurons:** Information processors; generate and propagate electrical signals.\n    \n- **Glia:** Maintain the environment that allows neurons to do their job; control metabolism, insulation, immune defense, and network regulation.","x":-6100,"y":-873,"width":709,"height":1100},
		{"id":"3f5021d473cc1987","type":"text","text":"Let's prep the content before we make a script. The content structure looks like this -\n1. Development and Characteristics of Neurons and Glia\n2. The nervous system is a \"clump\" of systematically connected neurons.\n3. Modern Research and Simulations have emulated this evolutionary process.\n4. (CTA to Blog about the history behind the emergence of modern neruoscience. Minimal content about the same in the video, just a hook and a CTA)","x":-6100,"y":-1200,"width":709,"height":231},
		{"id":"0c5189c9b1e95a73","type":"text","text":"# Neurons","x":-1845,"y":80,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"0d98f48e184675a3","type":"text","text":"### The Cytoskeleton Determines Cell Shape","x":-3667,"y":1490,"width":508,"height":80},
		{"id":"359be101fa7e70d1","type":"text","text":"### Neurofilaments","x":-3538,"y":1930,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"73ec2a0e93e91549","type":"text","text":"### Microtubules","x":-4000,"y":1930,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"69697f1422d4d523","type":"text","text":"![[image-38.png]]","x":-4569,"y":890,"width":694,"height":670},
		{"id":"136a1e3f8c40e90c","type":"text","text":"### **Hania Rani – “F Major”**\n\nThis is the **closest modern equivalent**.\n\n- Sparse\n    \n- Quiet harmonic motion\n    \n- Slightly unresolved\n    \n- Thinks more than it feels\n    \n\nIf Wyoming 307 had a European cousin, this is it.\n\n---\n\n### **Brian Eno – “1/1” (from _Music for Airports_)**\n\nThis is _foundational_.\n\nWhy it matches:\n\n- Non-intrusive\n    \n- Repetitive without boredom\n    \n- Creates a mental “workspace”\n    \n\nGrant’s Graph Duality video absolutely lives in this lineage.\n\n---\n\n### **Harold Budd – “Bismillahi ’Rrahmani ’Rrahim”**\n\nExtremely underrated.\n\n- Long decay\n    \n- Floating notes\n    \n- Almost no rhythm\n    \n\nFeels like watching ideas form.\n\n---\n\n## **American Minimalist Piano (Very Wyoming)**\n\n### **William Basinski – “Melancholia I”**\n\nIf you want:\n\n> “This system emerged slowly over time”\n\nThis works beautifully under network visuals.\n\n---\n\n### **Terry Riley – “In C” (piano excerpts only)**\n\nUse carefully.\n\n- Repetition\n    \n- Mathematical feel\n    \n- Intellectual curiosity over emotion\n    \n\n---\n\n## **Modern YouTube-Safe Equivalents**\n\nIf licensing matters.\n\n### **Chris Zabriskie**\n\n- **“I Am a Man Who Will Fight for Your Honor”**\n    \n- **“Divider”**\n    \n\nExtremely common in math / philosophy YouTube for a reason.\n\n---\n\n### **Kai Engel**\n\n- **“Moon”**\n    \n- **“September”**\n    \n\nA bit cleaner than Wyoming 307, but same calm cognition.\n\n---\n\n## **If I Had to Pick One for _Your_ Video**\n\nGiven your tone, Manim visuals, and neuroscience framing:\n\n**Brian Eno – “1/1”**\n\nWhy:\n\n- It _disappears_ under narration\n    \n- It does not emotionalize the content\n    \n- It invites sustained attention\n    \n\nSecond choice:  \n**Hania Rani – “F Major”** if you want slightly more texture.\n\n---\n\n\n\n## Intro - Dreaming in Color - Holly Jones","x":-6075,"y":300,"width":660,"height":1660},
		{"id":"1dfd070654bcd92f","type":"text","text":"Okay let's see, taking inspiration from you and going lecture by lecture,\nL1: Introduction to Neuroscience - Neurons and Glia where,\n I discuss the development and the overall characteristics of these cells, both neurons and glia.\n Put an intuitive idea that the whole nervous system is a \"clump\" of systematically connected cells and wires made of neurons made through millions of years of tinkering by evolution gave us this structure today what is known as the brain.\n* Interesting research that has been done with growing neurons artifically and visualising them, etc.\nFinally add a written blog of how the field emerged, discoveries and revelations that led to modern Neuroscience\n\n\nConnectomics\n\nMost of you must have heard of the study of economics. The wikipedia definition of which says \n\n\" ... \"\n\nand \n\nA standard textbook of economics says\n\n\" ... \" \n\nThe way I like to describe it in a much more abstract and general sense is that\n\n\"Economics is the study of  getting the most results while spending the least energy, in other words, being as lazy as you can\"\n\nThis isn't really being lazy for the sake of.. however you'd benefit from that\n\nIt's to make the system as stable as you can\n...Plus, why not avoid some work of you can just avoid it?\n\n\n\n\nHere in Neuroscience and the connection of neurons, we discuss what is called \"Connectomics\"","x":-6183,"y":2030,"width":875,"height":705}
	],
